<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sea Peoples</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1177 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sea Peoples were migrating confederacies of seafaring raiders who attacked the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>. Egyptian records describe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a> defeating them in land and sea battles around 1177 BCE. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> identify them as immediate agents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> collapse that destroyed the Hittite state, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> survived in weakened form.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sea-peoples.jpg" alt="Sea Peoples" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artistUnknown artist in pay of Ramesses III · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medinet_Habu_Ramses_III._Tempel_Nordostwand_Abzeichnung_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
